<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · region</p><h1 class="enc-title">Western New Guinea</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as West New Guinea</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-guinea">New Guinea</a> is the western half of the island of New Guinea, administered by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/indonesia">Indonesia</a> since 1963 and now divided into several Papuan provinces. Ruled by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-the-netherlands">Netherlands</a> as part of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/east-indies">East Indies</a> and, from 1949 to 1962, as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/netherlands-new-guinea">Netherlands New Guinea</a>, it passed to Indonesia after the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-york">New York</a> Agreement and a disputed vote of selected representatives in 1969, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/act-of-free-choice">Act of Free Choice</a>. A separatist movement has continued since the 1960s.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/western-new-guinea.jpg" alt="Western New Guinea" width="320" height="160" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Mandavi · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:LocationWestPapua.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>The region covers 412,214.61 square kilometres, 1,200 kilometres from east to west and 736 kilometres from north to south, and includes the Biak and Raja Ampat archipelagoes. It is largely rainforest, home to traditional peoples such as the Dani of the Baliem Valley, and its largest city is <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jayapura">Jayapura</a>. Names carry political meaning: Papua is the official name in the ISO standard, while independence activists say West Papua. Over the centuries it has also been called West New Guinea, West Irian and Irian Jaya.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>Papuan habitation is estimated to have begun more than 50,000 years ago. Research indicates that the highlands were an early and independent centre of agriculture, and the banana has been cultivated there for at least 7,000 years. Austronesian peoples settled the area at least 3,000 years ago, especially around Cenderawasih Bay. Trade with neighbouring Indonesian islands is documented as early as the seventh century, and from the Moluccas Muslim merchants built trading ties with the coast by the 17th century. Knowledge of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iron-technology">ironworking</a> reached the region around the 16th century.</p><p>In 1606 Luís Vaz de Torres reached the western part of the island and claimed it for the King of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a>. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch">Dutch</a> later claimed the region and made it part of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/east-indies">Dutch East Indies</a>, and began missionary work in the nineteenth century. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/japan">Japan</a> occupied the northern coast in 1942, and Dutch guerrillas under Mauritz Christiaan Kokkelink resisted behind its lines. In 1944 Jan van Eechoud set up a school for administrators at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jayapura">Hollandia</a>, now Jayapura, whose graduates went on to found nationalist movements both for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/indonesia">Indonesia</a> and for Papuan independence.</p><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sukarno">Sukarno</a> made the takeover of the territory a focus of his struggle against <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch">Dutch</a> imperialism. In December 1961 he created a Supreme Operations Command, and in January 1962 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/suharto">Suharto</a> was appointed to lead Operation Mandala. Infiltrations by sea and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/parachute">parachute</a> drops met difficult terrain and Papuan fighters; of 1,429 paratroopers, 216 were killed or missing and 296 captured. The Dutch transferred the region in 1962 to a United Nations Temporary Executive Authority, which passed it to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/indonesia">Indonesia</a>, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/act-of-free-choice">Act of Free Choice</a> followed in 1969.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/indonesia">Indonesia</a> granted the region Special Autonomy status in 2001 and divided it in 2003 into Papua and West Papua provinces, to which Central Papua, Highland Papua, South Papua and Southwest Papua were added in 2022. The 2020 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/census">census</a> counted 5,437,775 people, most of them indigenous. The official language is Indonesian, with Papuan Malay the most used <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lingua-franca">lingua franca</a>, and estimates of the local languages range from 200 to over 700. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/christianity">Christianity</a> is the predominant religion, followed by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/islam">Islam</a>. The main industries are agriculture, fishing, oil production and mining.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Western New Guinea” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The Netherlands transferred sovereignty on 27 December 1949, and <b>Western New Guinea</b> followed in 1962–1963.</blockquote><a href="/empire/dutch-empire">Dutch Empire · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li><li><blockquote>After Indonesian independence Sultan Zainal Abidin Syah supported Indonesia's claim to <b>West New Guinea</b> and became its first governor.</blockquote><a href="/state/tidore-sultanate">Tidore Sultanate</a></li></ul></section>
